// 2021 GS1 (Prelims) Set A — Transcribed from "E:\UPSC question paper\2021\2021.docx"
// Contains question, choices (A, B, C, D), answer, explanation, and upsc review points
export const paper = { year: 2021, name: 'GS1', set: 'A' };

export const questions = [
{"no":1,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nThe Governor of the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) is appointed by the Central Government.\nCertain provisions in the Constitution of India give the Central Government the right to issue directions to the RBI in public interest.\nThe Governor of the RBI draws his power from the RBI Act.\nWhich of the above statements are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["RBI","RBI Act","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Correct: The RBI Governor is appointed by the Central Government.\nStatement 2 – Incorrect: The RBI's statutory framework is primarily governed by the Reserve Bank of India Act, 1934, not by constitutional provisions giving such general directions.\nStatement 3 – Correct: The Governor derives statutory powers from the RBI Act, 1934.","revisionPoints":"RBI established: 1935.\nStatutory basis: RBI Act, 1934.\nRBI was nationalised in 1949.\nRBI is India's central bank.\nGovernor is appointed by the Central Government."},
{"no":2,"text":"With reference to casual workers employed in India, consider the following statements:\nAll casual workers are entitled for Employees Provident Fund coverage.\nAll casual workers are entitled for regular working hours and overtime payment.\nThe government can by a notification specify that an establishment or industry shall pay wages only through its bank account.\nWhich of the above statements are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Labour & Employment","tags":["Casual Workers","EPF","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Incorrect: EPF coverage is subject to the applicable statutory conditions; it is not automatically applicable to every casual worker.\nStatement 2 – Correct: Labour regulations provide protections concerning working hours and overtime.\nStatement 3 – Correct: The government can prescribe payment of wages through banking channels by notification.","revisionPoints":"Casual employment ≠ automatic universal EPF coverage.\nLabour laws regulate working hours, wages and overtime.\nWage payment can be regulated through specified banking mechanisms.\nDistinguish employment category from social-security coverage."},
{"no":3,"text":"Which among the following steps is most likely to be taken at the time of an economic recession?","A":"Cut in tax rates accompanied by increase in interest rate","B":"Increase in expenditure on public projects","C":"Increase in tax rates accompanied by reduction of interest rate","D":"Reduction of expenditure on public projects","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Economic Recession","Fiscal Policy","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"During a recession, aggregate demand tends to weaken.\nGovernment can use expansionary fiscal policy.\nIncreasing expenditure on public projects creates demand and employment.\nSuch spending can have a multiplier effect on economic activity.","revisionPoints":"Recession → weak economic activity/demand.\nExpansionary fiscal policy → higher government expenditure / lower taxes.\nContractionary fiscal policy → lower expenditure / higher taxes.\nPublic investment can support employment and aggregate demand."},
{"no":4,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nOther things remaining unchanged, market demand for a good might increase if:\nPrice of its substitute increases.\nPrice of its complement increases.\nThe good is an inferior good and income of the consumers increases.\nIts price falls.\nWhich of the above statements are correct?","A":"1 and 4 only","B":"2, 3 and 4","C":"1, 3 and 4","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Market Demand","Substitute Goods","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Correct: If the price of a substitute rises, consumers may shift toward the concerned good.\nStatement 2 – Incorrect: A rise in the price of a complement generally reduces demand for the good.\nStatement 3 – Incorrect: For an inferior good, an increase in income generally reduces demand.\nStatement 4 – Correct: A fall in the good's own price generally increases quantity demanded.","revisionPoints":"Substitute price ↑ → demand for concerned good ↑.\nComplement price ↑ → demand ↓.\nInferior good: income ↑ → demand generally ↓.\nOwn-price change causes a movement along the demand curve."},
{"no":5,"text":"With reference to Urban Cooperative Banks in India, consider the following statements:\nThey are supervised and regulated by local boards set up by the State Governments.\nThey can issue equity shares and preference shares.\nThey were brought under the purview of the Banking Regulation Act, 1949 through an Amendment in 1966.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["Urban Cooperative Banks","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Incorrect: UCB regulation is not simply through local boards established by State Governments.\nStatement 2 – Correct: Urban Cooperative Banks can issue equity and preference shares subject to applicable regulations.\nStatement 3 – Correct: UCBs were brought under the Banking Regulation Act framework through the 1966 amendment.","revisionPoints":"UCBs combine cooperative structure + banking functions.\nBanking Regulation Act, 1949 became applicable to cooperative banks through the 1966 amendment.\nRBI has an important regulatory role in banking functions.\nCooperative aspects can also involve State/other authorities."},
{"no":6,"text":"Indian Government Bond Yields are influenced by which of the following?\nActions of the United States Federal Reserve.\nActions of the Reserve Bank of India.\nInflation and short-term interest rates.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Financial Markets","tags":["Government Bond Yields","US Federal Reserve","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"US Federal Reserve actions influence global interest rates and capital flows.\nRBI monetary policy affects domestic interest rates and bond markets.\nInflation expectations influence required returns and bond yields.\nShort-term interest rates also influence the yield curve.","revisionPoints":"Bond yield and bond price generally move in opposite directions.\nHigher inflation expectations → generally higher yields.\nRBI policy affects domestic bond yields.\nUS Fed decisions can affect emerging-market capital flows."},
{"no":7,"text":"Consider the following:\nForeign currency convertible bonds\nForeign institutional investment with certain conditions\nGlobal Depository Receipts\nNon-resident external deposits\nWhich of the above can be included in Foreign Direct Investments?","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"3 only","C":"2 and 4","D":"1 and 4","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Financial Markets","tags":["Foreign Direct Investment","FCCBs","GDRs","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"FCCBs can be converted into equity and can form part of foreign investment.\nCertain foreign institutional investments can qualify as foreign investment subject to prescribed conditions.\nGDRs represent shares of an Indian company issued through an overseas depositary mechanism.\nNRE deposits are bank deposits of non-residents, not FDI.","revisionPoints":"FDI → investment involving a lasting interest/control in an enterprise.\nFCCB → debt instrument with conversion feature.\nGDR → represents underlying shares.\nNRE deposit → bank deposit, not FDI."},
{"no":8,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nThe effect of devaluation of a currency is that it necessarily:\nImproves the competitiveness of the domestic exports in the foreign markets.\nIncreases the foreign value of domestic currency.\nImproves the trade balance.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2","C":"3 only","D":"2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Currency Devaluation","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Correct: Devaluation makes domestic goods relatively cheaper in foreign-currency terms, potentially improving export competitiveness.\nStatement 2 – Incorrect: Devaluation means a reduction in the value of the domestic currency.\nStatement 3 – Incorrect: Trade balance does not necessarily improve immediately; import costs and elasticities matter.","revisionPoints":"Devaluation → official reduction in currency value under a fixed/managed exchange-rate system.\nDepreciation → market-driven fall in currency value.\nDevaluation can make exports cheaper and imports costlier.\nTrade-balance improvement depends on factors such as price elasticities."},
{"no":9,"text":"Which one of the following effects of creation of black money in India has been the main cause of worry to the Government of India?","A":"Diversion of resources to the purchase of real estate and investment in luxury housing","B":"Investment in unproductive activities and purchase of precious stones, jewellery, gold, etc.","C":"Large donations to political parties and growth of regionalism","D":"Loss of revenue to the State Exchequer due to tax evasion","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Taxation","tags":["Black Money","Tax Evasion","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Black money commonly involves income or transactions concealed from tax authorities.\nTax evasion reduces government revenue.\nLower revenue can constrain public expenditure and fiscal capacity.","revisionPoints":"Black money → income/wealth concealed from lawful taxation or reporting.\nTax evasion → illegal concealment/under-reporting.\nTax avoidance → generally refers to reducing tax liability through legal means.\nBlack money can also affect investment patterns and resource allocation."},
{"no":10,"text":"Which one of the following is likely to be the most inflationary in its effects?","A":"Repayment of public debt","B":"Borrowing from the public to finance a budget deficit","C":"Borrowing from the banks to finance a budget deficit","D":"Creation of new money to finance a budget deficit","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Finance","tags":["Budget Deficit","Deficit Financing","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Creation of new money directly increases the money supply.\nIf output does not increase correspondingly, additional money can generate excess demand.\nExcess demand can create demand-pull inflationary pressure.","revisionPoints":"Deficit financing through money creation can be highly inflationary.\nInflation depends on the relationship between money supply, demand and output.\nPublic borrowing does not necessarily create the same immediate monetary expansion.\nExcess aggregate demand → demand-pull inflation."},
{"no":11,"text":"The money multiplier in an economy increases with which one of the following?","A":"Increase in the Cash Reserve Ratio in the banks","B":"Increase in the Statutory Liquidity Ratio in the banks","C":"Increase in the banking habit of the people","D":"Increase in the population of the country","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["Money Multiplier","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Greater use of banking channels means people keep more money within the banking system.\nThis allows banks to use deposits for lending and deposit creation.\nHigher CRR or SLR generally reduces the capacity of banks to create credit.","revisionPoints":"Money multiplier is linked to the process of deposit and credit creation.\nCRR ↑ → lending capacity generally ↓.\nSLR ↑ → lending capacity generally ↓.\nGreater banking habit → greater potential for deposit multiplication."},
{"no":12,"text":"With reference to Indian economy, demand-pull inflation can be caused/increased by which of the following?\nExpansionary policies\nFiscal stimulus\nInflation-indexing wages\nHigher purchasing power\nRising interest rates","A":"1, 2 and 4 only","B":"3, 4 and 5 only","C":"1, 2, 3 and 5 only","D":"1, 2, 3, 4 and 5","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Demand-pull Inflation","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Expansionary policies increase aggregate demand.\nFiscal stimulus can increase spending and demand.\nHigher purchasing power can increase consumption demand.\nInflation-indexing wages is more closely associated with cost/wage pressures rather than being a direct cause of demand-pull inflation.\nRising interest rates generally reduce borrowing and aggregate demand.","revisionPoints":"Demand-pull inflation → too much aggregate demand relative to supply.\nExpansionary fiscal policy → demand ↑.\nExpansionary monetary policy → demand ↑.\nHigher interest rates generally → borrowing/spending ↓."},
{"no":13,"text":"With reference to India, consider the following statements:\nRetail investors through demat account can invest in Treasury Bills and Government of India Debt Bonds in primary market.\nThe Negotiated Dealing System-Order Matching is a government securities trading platform of the Reserve Bank of India.\nThe Central Depository Services Ltd. is jointly promoted by the Reserve Bank of India and the Bombay Stock Exchange.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2","C":"3 only","D":"2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Financial Markets","tags":["NDS-OM","Central Depository Services","Treasury Bills","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Correct: Retail investors can participate in the primary market for government securities through the prescribed mechanism.\nStatement 2 – Correct: NDS-OM is an electronic platform for trading in government securities.\nStatement 3 – Incorrect: CDSL was promoted by institutions associated with the securities market, not jointly by RBI and BSE in the manner stated.","revisionPoints":"NDS-OM → government securities trading platform.\nGovernment securities include Treasury Bills and dated securities.\nCDSL → depository infrastructure for securities.\nRBI plays a major role in the government securities market."},
{"no":14,"text":"With reference to \"WaterCredit\", consider the following statements:\nIt puts microfinance tools to work in the water and sanitation sector.\nIt is a global initiative launched under the aegis of the World Health Organization and the World Bank.\nIt aims to enable the poor people to meet their water needs without depending on subsidies.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["WaterCredit","Microfinance","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Correct: WaterCredit applies microfinance approaches to water and sanitation needs.\nStatement 2 – Incorrect: It was not launched under the joint aegis of WHO and World Bank.\nStatement 3 – Correct: The model seeks to help low-income communities finance water and sanitation solutions rather than depend entirely on subsidies.","revisionPoints":"WaterCredit → microfinance + water/sanitation.\nFocuses on access to safe water and sanitation.\nFinancial inclusion can support basic infrastructure access.\nDo not confuse WaterCredit with a WHO/World Bank programme."},
{"no":15,"text":"In India, the central bank's function as the \"lender of last resort\" usually refers to which of the following?\nLending to trade and industry bodies when they fail to borrow from other sources.\nProviding liquidity to banks having a temporary crisis.\nLending to governments to finance budgetary deficits.","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 only","C":"2 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["Lender of Last Resort","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"A central bank acts as lender of last resort when banks face temporary liquidity problems.\nIt provides emergency liquidity when normal sources of funds are unavailable.\nIt is not primarily a facility for financing trade/industry or government budget deficits.","revisionPoints":"Lender of Last Resort → liquidity support to banks/financial institutions.\nObjective → maintain financial stability.\nLiquidity crisis ≠ necessarily insolvency.\nRBI is India's central bank and lender of last resort."},
{"no":16,"text":"\"R2 Code of Practices\" constitutes a tool available for promotion of the adoption of:","A":"Environmentally responsible practices in electronics recycling industry","B":"Ecological management of Wetlands of International Importance under the Ramsar Convention","C":"Sustainable practices in the cultivation of agricultural crops in degraded lands","D":"Environmental Impact Assessment in the exploitation of natural resources","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Environmental Technology","tags":["R2 Code of Practices","E-waste Recycling","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"R2 refers to Responsible Recycling.\nIt provides standards/practices for responsible management of used and end-of-life electronics.\nIt focuses on environmentally sound recycling and management of electronic waste.","revisionPoints":"R2 → Responsible Recycling.\nAssociated with electronics recycling/e-waste management.\nFocus → environmental, health and safety practices.\nE-waste management includes recovery, reuse, recycling and safe disposal."},
{"no":17,"text":"Why is there a concern about copper smelting plants?\nThey may release lethal quantities of carbon monoxide into the environment.\nThe copper slag can cause the leaching of some heavy metals into the environment.\nThey may release sulphur dioxide as a pollutant.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["Copper Smelting","Sulphur Dioxide","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Incorrect: Carbon monoxide is not the principal environmental concern highlighted here.\nStatement 2 – Correct: Copper slag can contain heavy metals that may leach into the environment.\nStatement 3 – Correct: Copper smelting can release sulphur dioxide, especially when sulphide ores are processed.","revisionPoints":"Copper smelting → metal extraction from copper ores/concentrates.\nMajor pollutant concern → SO₂.\nCopper slag → may contain heavy metals.\nHeavy-metal leaching can contaminate soil and water."},
{"no":18,"text":"With reference to furnace oil, consider the following statements:\nIt is a product of oil refineries.\nSome industries use it to generate power.\nIts use causes sulphur emissions into environment.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["Furnace Oil","Sulphur Emissions","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Correct: Furnace oil is a petroleum product obtained during refinery processing.\nStatement 2 – Correct: It has been used as an industrial fuel for boilers, furnaces and power generation.\nStatement 3 – Correct: Sulphur-containing furnace oil can produce SO₂ emissions when burned.","revisionPoints":"Furnace oil → petroleum-derived industrial fuel.\nUsed in boilers, furnaces and industrial heating.\nSulphur in fuel → sulphur oxides on combustion.\nSO₂ contributes to air pollution and acid deposition."},
{"no":19,"text":"What is blue carbon?","A":"Carbon captured by oceans and coastal ecosystems","B":"Carbon sequestered in forest biomass and agricultural soils","C":"Carbon contained in petroleum and natural gas","D":"Carbon present in atmosphere","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["Blue Carbon","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Blue carbon refers to carbon captured and stored by marine and coastal ecosystems.\nImportant blue-carbon ecosystems include mangroves, seagrass meadows and tidal/salt marshes.\nThese ecosystems can store significant amounts of carbon in biomass and sediments.","revisionPoints":"Blue carbon → marine/coastal ecosystems.\nMajor ecosystems → mangroves, seagrasses, salt marshes.\nGreen carbon → broadly associated with terrestrial vegetation/forests.\nCoastal ecosystems have both climate-mitigation and biodiversity importance."},
{"no":20,"text":"In nature, which of the following is/are most likely to be found surviving on a surface without soil?\nFern\nLichen\nMoss\nMushroom","A":"1 and 4 only","B":"2 only","C":"2 and 3","D":"1, 3 and 4","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Lichen","Moss","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Lichens can colonize bare surfaces such as rocks because they do not require developed soil.\nMosses can also grow on bare surfaces and contribute to soil formation.\nFerns generally require more developed growing conditions.\nMushrooms are fungi and typically depend on organic matter/substrate rather than simply a bare soil-less surface.","revisionPoints":"Lichens → pioneer organisms on bare rock surfaces.\nLichens contribute to weathering and soil formation.\nMosses can colonize relatively exposed surfaces.\nPioneer species help initiate ecological succession."},
{"no":21,"text":"Which one of the following is used in preparing a natural mosquito repellent?","A":"Congress grass","B":"Elephant grass","C":"Lemongrass","D":"Nut grass","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Lemongrass","Mosquito Repellent","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Lemongrass contains aromatic compounds that can help repel mosquitoes.\nIts essential oil is used in several natural insect-repellent formulations.","revisionPoints":"Lemongrass → aromatic grass.\nEssential oils from plants can have insect-repellent properties.\nPlant-based repellents are alternatives to synthetic chemical repellents."},
{"no":22,"text":"Consider the following kinds of organisms:\nCopepods\nCyanobacteria\nDiatoms\nForaminifera\nWhich of the above are primary producers in the food chains of oceans?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 and 3","C":"3 and 4","D":"1 and 4","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Primary Producers","Phytoplankton","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Cyanobacteria are photosynthetic organisms and can act as primary producers.\nDiatoms are photosynthetic phytoplankton and major marine primary producers.\nCopepods are generally consumers.\nForaminifera are generally consumers.","revisionPoints":"Primary producers → convert inorganic matter into organic matter, mainly through photosynthesis.\nMarine primary producers → phytoplankton.\nDiatoms → major group of marine phytoplankton.\nCyanobacteria → photosynthetic prokaryotes."},
{"no":23,"text":"Consider the following animals:\nHedgehog\nMarmot\nPangolin\nTo reduce the chance of being captured by predators, which of the above organisms roll up and protect their vulnerable parts?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 only","C":"3 only","D":"1 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Pangolin","Hedgehog","Anti-predator Behaviour","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Hedgehog curls into a ball and exposes its spines to predators.\nPangolin curls its body into a protective ball, using its scales as armour.\nMarmot does not use this rolling-up defence mechanism.","revisionPoints":"Hedgehog → defensive spines + curling.\nPangolin → overlapping scales + curling.\nDefensive adaptations help prey reduce vulnerability to predators.\nBehavioural and physical adaptations often work together."},
{"no":24,"text":"With reference to the \"New York Declaration on Forests\", which of the following statements are correct?\nIt was first endorsed at the United Nations Climate Summit in 2014.\nIt endorses a global timeline to end the loss of forests.\nIt is a legally binding international declaration.\nIt is endorsed by governments, big companies and indigenous communities.\nIndia was one of the signatories at its inception.","A":"1, 2 and 4","B":"1, 3 and 5","C":"3 and 4","D":"2 and 5","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["New York Declaration on Forests","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Correct: The declaration was launched at the UN Climate Summit in 2014.\nStatement 2 – Correct: It established global goals for reducing and eventually ending deforestation.\nStatement 3 – Incorrect: It is not legally binding.\nStatement 4 – Correct: It involved governments, companies, civil society and indigenous/community groups.\nStatement 5 – Incorrect: India was not an original signatory at its inception.","revisionPoints":"New York Declaration on Forests → 2014.\nFocus → reducing deforestation and restoring degraded landscapes.\nIt is voluntary, not legally binding.\nInvolves multiple stakeholders beyond governments."},
{"no":25,"text":"Magnetite particles, suspected to cause neurodegenerative problems, are generated as environmental pollutants from which of the following?\nBrakes of motor vehicles\nEngines of motor vehicles\nMicrowave stoves within homes\nPower plants\nTelephone lines","A":"1, 2, 3 and 5 only","B":"1, 2 and 4 only","C":"3, 4 and 5 only","D":"1, 2, 3, 4 and 5","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["Magnetite Particles","Vehicular Pollution","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Magnetite nanoparticles can be generated through high-temperature combustion and friction processes.\nVehicle brakes can release metallic particles through friction.\nVehicle engines can generate particles during combustion and mechanical processes.\nPower plants can also be sources of magnetite-containing particulate pollution.\nTelephone lines are not a significant source in this context.","revisionPoints":"Magnetite → iron oxide, Fe₃O₄.\nUltrafine magnetic particles can be produced by combustion and friction.\nAirborne nanoparticles can enter the human respiratory system.\nPollution can arise from both transport and industrial sources."},
{"no":26,"text":"Which one of the following is a filter feeder?","A":"Catfish","B":"Octopus","C":"Oyster","D":"Pelican","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Filter Feeders","Oyster","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Oysters are filter feeders.\nThey draw water through their bodies and filter suspended food particles, especially plankton.\nThis also contributes to water filtration in aquatic ecosystems.","revisionPoints":"Filter feeding → obtaining food by filtering particles from water.\nOysters → filter-feed mainly on plankton and suspended organic matter.\nOther examples → mussels, clams and some whales.\nOysters are important components of coastal ecosystems."},
{"no":27,"text":"In case of which one of the following biogeochemical cycles, the weathering of rocks is the main source of release of nutrient into the cycle?","A":"Carbon cycle","B":"Nitrogen cycle","C":"Phosphorus cycle","D":"Sulphur cycle","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Phosphorus Cycle","Biogeochemical Cycles","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Phosphorus is mainly stored in rocks and mineral deposits.\nWeathering releases phosphate into soil and water.\nPlants absorb phosphate, after which it moves through food chains.","revisionPoints":"Phosphorus cycle → primarily a sedimentary cycle.\nMajor reservoir → rocks and sediments.\nWeathering → releases phosphate.\nUnlike carbon and nitrogen, phosphorus has no major gaseous atmospheric phase."},
{"no":28,"text":"Which of the following are detritivores?\nEarthworms\nJellyfish\nMillipedes\nSeahorses\nWoodlice","A":"1, 2 and 4 only","B":"2, 3, 4 and 5 only","C":"1, 3 and 5 only","D":"1, 2, 3, 4 and 5","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Detritivores","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Earthworms feed on decomposing organic matter and soil material.\nMillipedes commonly consume decaying plant material.\nWoodlice feed mainly on dead and decaying organic matter.\nJellyfish and seahorses are not detritivores.","revisionPoints":"Detritivores → organisms that consume dead and decaying organic matter.\nExamples → earthworms, millipedes, woodlice.\nDetritivores physically break down detritus.\nDecomposers such as bacteria and fungi chemically decompose organic matter."},
{"no":29,"text":"The \"Common Carbon Metric\", supported by UNEP, has been developed for:","A":"Assessing the carbon footprint of building operations around the world","B":"Enabling commercial farming entities around the world to enter carbon emission trading","C":"Enabling governments to assess the overall carbon footprint caused by their countries","D":"Assessing the overall carbon footprint caused by the use of fossil fuels by the world in a unit time","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["Common Carbon Metric","UNEP","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"The Common Carbon Metric was developed as a standardized approach to measure carbon emissions associated with building operations.\nIt supports comparison and assessment of energy use and emissions in buildings.","revisionPoints":"Common Carbon Metric → buildings.\nSupported by UNEP.\nFocuses on operational energy consumption and associated carbon emissions.\nBuildings are important contributors to global energy consumption and emissions."},
{"no":30,"text":"Which of the following have species that can establish symbiotic relationship with other organisms?\nCnidarians\nFungi\nProtozoa","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Symbiosis","Cnidarians","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Cnidarians such as corals can have symbiotic relationships with photosynthetic algae.\nFungi form mutualistic associations such as mycorrhizae and lichens.\nProtozoa can also form symbiotic relationships with other organisms.","revisionPoints":"Symbiosis → close biological association between different organisms.\nCoral–algal association → important example.\nMycorrhiza → fungus + plant roots.\nLichen → fungus + photosynthetic partner.\nSymbiosis can be mutualistic, commensal or parasitic."},
{"no":31,"text":"With reference to Chausath Yogini Temple situated near Morena, consider the following statements:\nIt is a circular temple built during the reign of Kachchhapaghata Dynasty.\nIt is the only circular temple built in India.\nIt was meant to promote the Vaishnava cult in the region.\nIts design has given rise to a popular belief that it was the inspiration behind the Indian Parliament building.","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 4","D":"2, 3 and 4","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Temples & Shrines","tags":["Chausath Yogini Temple","Kachchhapaghata Dynasty","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Correct: The Chausath Yogini Temple at Mitaoli is circular and associated with the Kachchhapaghata dynasty.\nStatement 2 – Incorrect: It is not the only circular temple in India.\nStatement 3 – Incorrect: The temple is associated with the Yogini/Shakta tradition, not the Vaishnava cult.\nStatement 4 – Correct: Its circular design has led to the popular belief that it inspired the design of the Indian Parliament building.","revisionPoints":"Location → Mitaoli, Morena, Madhya Pradesh.\nDedicated to the 64 Yoginis.\nCircular architectural plan.\nAssociated with Shakta/Tantric traditions.\nParliament-building connection is a popular belief, not an established architectural fact."},
{"no":32,"text":"Which one of the following ancient towns is well-known for its elaborate system of water harvesting and management by building a series of dams and channelizing water into connected reservoirs?","A":"Dholavira","B":"Kalibangan","C":"Rakhigarhi","D":"Ropar","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Dholavira","Water Harvesting","Harappan Civilization","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Dholavira was a major Harappan site in present-day Gujarat.\nIts inhabitants developed an advanced system of dams, reservoirs, channels and water storage structures.\nThis was particularly important in its relatively arid environment.","revisionPoints":"Dholavira → Indus Valley/Harappan civilisation.\nLocated in Kutch, Gujarat.\nFamous for sophisticated water management.\nUNESCO World Heritage Site.\nShows advanced urban planning and adaptation to water scarcity."},
{"no":33,"text":"In the first quarter of the seventeenth century, in which of the following was/were the factory/factories of the English East India Company located?\nBroach\nChicacole\nTrichinopoly","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2","C":"3 only","D":"2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["English East India Company","Factories","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Broach (Bharuch) was an important early English East India Company trading centre.\nChicacole and Trichinopoly were not among the relevant English factories during the specified period.","revisionPoints":"English East India Company → established in 1600.\nEarly factories were established mainly for trade and commercial activity.\nBroach/Bharuch → important port and textile-trading centre.\nEarly European factories were concentrated near important ports and commercial centres."},
{"no":34,"text":"From the decline of Guptas until the rise of Harshavardhana in the early seventh century, which of the following kingdoms were holding power in Northern India?\nThe Guptas of Magadha\nThe Paramaras of Malwa\nThe Pushyabhutis of Thanesar\nThe Maukharis of Kanauj\nThe Yadavas of Devagiri\nThe Maitrakas of Valabhi","A":"1, 2 and 5","B":"1, 3, 4 and 6","C":"2, 3 and 4","D":"5 and 6","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Guptas","Pushyabhutis","Maukharis","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Guptas of Magadha continued in a reduced form after the decline of the imperial Guptas.\nPushyabhutis of Thanesar rose to prominence and produced Harshavardhana.\nMaukharis of Kanauj were an important north Indian power.\nMaitrakas of Valabhi controlled parts of western India.\nParamaras and Yadavas belong to later periods.","revisionPoints":"Post-Gupta period → political fragmentation in northern India.\nPushyabhuti dynasty → Thanesar.\nHarshavardhana → Pushyabhuti ruler.\nMaukharis → Kanauj.\nMaitrakas → Valabhi/Gujarat region.\nParamaras and Yadavas rose prominently in later centuries."},
{"no":35,"text":"According to Portuguese writer Nuniz, the women in Vijayanagara Empire were expert in which of the following areas?\nWrestling\nAstrology\nAccounting\nSoothsaying","A":"1, 2 and 3 only","B":"1, 3 and 4 only","C":"2 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Vijayanagara Empire","Nuniz","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Portuguese traveller Fernao Nuniz described the varied roles and skills of women in Vijayanagara.\nHis account mentions women participating in activities including wrestling, astrology, accounting and soothsaying.","revisionPoints":"Nuniz → Portuguese traveller associated with Vijayanagara.\nForeign travellers provide important accounts of Vijayanagara society.\nWomen could participate in various professional and cultural activities.\nVijayanagara → major South Indian empire centred at Hampi."},
{"no":36,"text":"With reference to Madanapalle of Andhra Pradesh, which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"Pingali Venkayya designed the tricolour Indian National Flag here.","B":"Pattabhi Sitaramaiah led the Quit India Movement of Andhra region from here.","C":"Rabindranath Tagore translated the National Anthem from Bengali to English here.","D":"Madame Blavatsky and Colonel Olcott set up headquarters of Theosophical Society first here.","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Madanapalle","National Anthem","Tagore","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Madanapalle is associated with Rabindranath Tagore.\nTagore stayed there in 1919 and translated Jana Gana Mana into English.\nThe English version became known as \"The Morning Song of India.\"","revisionPoints":"Madanapalle → Andhra Pradesh.\nRabindranath Tagore → associated with translation of the National Anthem into English at Madanapalle.\nPingali Venkayya → associated with the design of the Indian national flag.\nTheosophical Society headquarters → Adyar, Chennai."},
{"no":37,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\nHistorical Place\nWell-known for\n1. Burzahom\nRock-cut shrines\n2. Chandraketugarh\nTerracotta art\n3. Ganeshwar\nCopper artefacts\nWhich of the pairs given above is/are correctly matched?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2","C":"3 only","D":"2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Burzahom","Chandraketugarh","Ganeshwar","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Burzahom – Incorrect: Burzahom in Kashmir is known for Neolithic remains and pit dwellings, not rock-cut shrines.\nChandraketugarh – Correct: Famous for archaeological remains and terracotta art.\nGaneshwar – Correct: Known for significant copper artefacts.","revisionPoints":"Burzahom → Kashmir Valley, Neolithic culture.\nChandraketugarh → West Bengal, terracotta/art objects.\nGaneshwar → Rajasthan, copper-related archaeological culture.\nArchaeological sites can be identified through their distinctive material remains."},
{"no":38,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nIt was during the reign of Iltutmish that Chengiz Khan reached the Indus in pursuit of the fugitive Khwarezm prince.\nIt was during the reign of Muhammad bin Tughluq that Taimur occupied Multan and crossed the Indus.\nIt was during the reign of Deva Raya II of Vijayanagara Empire that Vasco da Gama reached the coast of Kerala.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2","C":"3 only","D":"2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Iltutmish","Chengiz Khan","Taimur","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Correct: During Iltutmish's reign, the Mongol ruler Chengiz Khan reached the Indus while pursuing Jalal-ud-din Mangburni.\nStatement 2 – Incorrect: Timur invaded India during the reign of Nasir-ud-din Mahmud Shah Tughlaq, not Muhammad bin Tughluq.\nStatement 3 – Incorrect: Vasco da Gama reached Kerala in 1498, during the reign of Saluva Narasimha/early Tuluva period, not Deva Raya II.","revisionPoints":"Iltutmish → Delhi Sultanate, Slave/Mamluk dynasty.\nChengiz Khan → Mongol ruler.\nTimur's invasion of India → 1398.\nVasco da Gama reached Calicut in 1498.\nChronology is crucial in medieval-history questions."},
{"no":39,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nSt. Francis Xavier was one of the founding members of the Jesuit Order.\nSt. Francis Xavier died in Goa and a church is dedicated to him there.\nThe Feast of St. Francis Xavier is celebrated in Goa each year.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["St. Francis Xavier","Jesuit Order","Goa","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Correct: Francis Xavier was one of the founding members of the Society of Jesus (Jesuits).\nStatement 2 – Incorrect: He died on Shangchuan Island in China, not Goa.\nHis body was later brought to Goa and is preserved at the Basilica of Bom Jesus.\nStatement 3 – Correct: His feast is celebrated annually in Goa.","revisionPoints":"St. Francis Xavier → Jesuit missionary.\nSociety of Jesus founded in 1540.\nXavier died in 1552 on Shangchuan Island.\nHis remains are associated with the Basilica of Bom Jesus, Goa.\nFeast → 3 December."},
{"no":40,"text":"With reference to the history of ancient India, which of the following statements is/are correct?\nMitakshara was the civil law for upper castes and Dayabhaga was the civil law for lower castes.\nIn the Mitakshara system, the sons can claim right to the property during the lifetime of the father, whereas in the Dayabhaga system, it is only after the death of the father that the sons can claim right to the property.\nThe Mitakshara system deals with matters related to property held by male members only of a family, whereas the Dayabhaga system deals with property held by both male and female members of a family.","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Mitakshara","Dayabhaga","Hindu Law","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Incorrect: Mitakshara and Dayabhaga were not simply laws divided according to caste.\nStatement 2 – Correct: Under Mitakshara, the son's interest in ancestral property arises by birth; under Dayabhaga, inheritance generally arises after the father's death.\nStatement 3 – Incorrect: The distinction is primarily about inheritance and coparcenary rights, not simply male versus female property.","revisionPoints":"Mitakshara → associated with Vijnaneshwara.\nMitakshara → right in ancestral property arises by birth.\nDayabhaga → associated with Jimutavahana.\nDayabhaga → inheritance generally opens after father's death.\nDayabhaga was particularly influential in Bengal and Assam.\nThese systems relate to Hindu inheritance/property law, not a simple upper-caste/lower-caste division.\nUPSC 2021 – Questions 41–60"},
{"no":41,"text":"With reference to the history of ancient India, Bahubali, Hastimalla and Kshemeshvara were famous for:","A":"Jain monks","B":"Playwrights","C":"Temple architects","D":"Philosophers","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Bahubali","Playwrights","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Bahubali, Hastimalla and Kshemeshvara were known as playwrights.\nThe question tests identification of personalities from ancient/medieval Indian literary traditions.","revisionPoints":"Bahubali, Hastimalla and Kshemeshvara → playwrights.\nUPSC can ask about lesser-known personalities through their field of contribution.\nConnect personalities with literature, architecture, religion and philosophy."},
{"no":42,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nThe Montagu-Chelmsford Reforms of 1919 recommended granting voting rights to all the women above the age of 21.\nThe Government of India Act of 1935 gave women reserved seats in legislature.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Montagu-Chelmsford Reforms","Government of India Act 1935","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Incorrect: The Montagu-Chelmsford reforms did not provide universal voting rights to all women above 21.\nStatement 2 – Correct: The Government of India Act, 1935 provided for reserved seats for women in legislatures.","revisionPoints":"Montagu-Chelmsford Reforms → Government of India Act, 1919.\n1919 Act introduced dyarchy in provinces.\nGovernment of India Act, 1935 → provincial autonomy and expanded representation.\nWomen's political representation developed gradually during colonial constitutional reforms."},
{"no":43,"text":"With reference to 8th August, 1942 in Indian history, which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"The Quit India Resolution was adopted by the AICC.","B":"The Viceroy's Executive Council was expanded to include more Indians.","C":"The Congress ministries resigned in seven provinces.","D":"Cripps proposed an Indian Union with full Dominion Status once the Second World War was over.","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Quit India Movement","AICC","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"On 8 August 1942, the All India Congress Committee (AICC) adopted the Quit India Resolution at Bombay.\nGandhi gave the call of \"Do or Die.\"\nThe movement demanded an immediate end to British rule in India.","revisionPoints":"Quit India Movement → 1942.\nAICC resolution → 8 August 1942.\nGandhi's slogan → \"Do or Die.\"\nCripps Mission → 1942, before the Quit India Movement.\nCongress ministries resigned in 1939, following India's involvement in World War II without Indian consultation."},
{"no":44,"text":"Who among the following is associated with \"Songs from Prison\", a translation of ancient Indian religious lyrics in English?","A":"Bal Gangadhar Tilak","B":"Jawaharlal Nehru","C":"Mohandas Karamchand Gandhi","D":"Sarojini Naidu","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Mahatma Gandhi","Songs from Prison","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Songs from Prison is associated with Mahatma Gandhi.\nIt contains translations of devotional/religious verses that Gandhi worked on while imprisoned.","revisionPoints":"Gandhi → associated with Songs from Prison.\nGandhi's writings included political, social, ethical and religious themes.\nPrison writings are an important source for understanding the nationalist movement."},
{"no":45,"text":"With reference to medieval India, which one of the following is the correct sequence in ascending order in terms of size?","A":"Pargana → Sarkar → Suba","B":"Sarkar → Pargana → Suba","C":"Suba → Sarkar → Pargana","D":"Pargana → Suba → Sarkar","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Mughal Administration","Suba","Sarkar","Pargana","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Under the Mughal administrative system:\nPargana was a smaller administrative unit.\nSeveral parganas formed a Sarkar.\nSeveral sarkars formed a Suba.","revisionPoints":"Pargana → smaller unit.\nSarkar → collection of parganas.\nSuba → larger provincial unit.\nOverall hierarchy: Pargana → Sarkar → Suba."},
{"no":46,"text":"Who among the following was associated as Secretary with Hindu Female School which later came to be known as Bethune Female School?","A":"Annie Besant","B":"Debendranath Tagore","C":"Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar","D":"Sarojini Naidu","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Bethune Female School","Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar played an important role in the development of women's education in Bengal.\nHe was associated with the Hindu Female School, which later became associated with Bethune.","revisionPoints":"Vidyasagar → major social reformer of 19th-century Bengal.\nStrong advocate of women's education.\nAssociated with the movement for widow remarriage.\nBethune School → important institution in the history of women's education in India."},
{"no":47,"text":"In the context of Colonial India, Shah Nawaz Khan, Prem Kumar Sehgal and Gurbaksh Singh Dhillon are remembered as:","A":"Leaders of Swadeshi and Boycott Movement","B":"Members of the Interim Government in 1946","C":"Members of the Drafting Committee in the Constituent Assembly","D":"Officers of the Indian National Army","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Indian National Army","INA Trials","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Shah Nawaz Khan, Prem Kumar Sehgal and Gurbaksh Singh Dhillon were officers of the Indian National Army (INA).\nThey became prominent during the famous INA trials after World War II.","revisionPoints":"INA → associated with Subhas Chandra Bose.\nShah Nawaz Khan, Sehgal and Dhillon → INA officers.\nINA trials → held at the Red Fort, Delhi.\nINA trials became an important political development in the final phase of British rule."},
{"no":48,"text":"With reference to Indian history, which of the following statements is/are correct?\nThe Nizamat of Arcot emerged out of Hyderabad State.\nThe Mysore Kingdom emerged out of Vijayanagara Empire.\nRohilkhand Kingdom was formed out of the territories occupied by Ahmad Shah Durrani.","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 only","C":"2 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Nizamat of Arcot","Mysore Kingdom","Rohilkhand","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Incorrect: The Nizamat of Arcot developed as an autonomous political entity under the Carnatic Nawabs, not simply as a direct emergence from Hyderabad State.\nStatement 2 – Correct: The Mysore Kingdom rose in the political environment created by the decline of the Vijayanagara Empire.\nStatement 3 – Incorrect: Rohilkhand was associated with the Rohilla Afghans, not territories occupied by Ahmad Shah Durrani.","revisionPoints":"Decline of Vijayanagara → emergence of several regional powers.\nMysore became an important South Indian kingdom.\nArcot/Carnatic → important 18th-century regional polity.\nRohilkhand → associated with Rohilla Afghan chiefs."},
{"no":49,"text":"Which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"Ajanta Caves lie in the gorge of Waghora River.","B":"Sanchi Stupa lies in the gorge of Chambal River.","C":"Pandu-lena Cave Shrines lie in the gorge of Narmada River.","D":"Amaravati Stupa lies in the gorge of Godavari River.","ans":"A","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"UNESCO Heritage","tags":["Ajanta Caves","Waghora River","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Ajanta Caves are located along the Waghora River in Maharashtra.\nThe caves were excavated into a horseshoe-shaped cliff overlooking the river valley.","revisionPoints":"Ajanta → Maharashtra.\nRiver → Waghora.\nMainly associated with Buddhist cave architecture and paintings.\nSanchi → Madhya Pradesh.\nAmaravati → Andhra Pradesh, associated with the Krishna River region."},
{"no":50,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n21st February is declared to be the International Mother Language Day by UNICEF.\nThe demand that Bangla has to be one of the national languages was raised in the Constituent Assembly of Pakistan.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["International Mother Language Day","Bangladesh Liberation","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Incorrect: International Mother Language Day was proclaimed by UNESCO, not UNICEF.\nStatement 2 – Correct: The demand for recognition of Bangla/Bengali as a state language was raised in Pakistan's Constituent Assembly.","revisionPoints":"International Mother Language Day → 21 February.\nProclaimed by UNESCO in 1999.\nConnected historically with the Bengali Language Movement.\nBangladesh observes 21 February as Shaheed Dibosh.\nLanguage movement was an important factor in the development of Bengali nationalism."},
{"no":51,"text":"How is permaculture farming different from conventional chemical farming?\nPermaculture farming discourages monocultural practices but in conventional chemical farming, monoculture practices are predominant.\nConventional chemical farming can cause increase in soil salinity but the occurrence of such phenomenon is not observed in permaculture farming.\nConventional chemical farming is easily possible in semi-arid regions but permaculture farming is not so easily possible in such regions.\nPractice of mulching is very important in permaculture farming but not necessarily so in conventional chemical farming.","A":"1 and 3","B":"1, 2 and 4","C":"4 only","D":"2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Permaculture Farming","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Correct: Permaculture generally promotes diversity and discourages monoculture.\nStatement 2 – Correct: Intensive conventional farming practices can contribute to soil degradation and salinity under certain conditions.\nStatement 3 – Incorrect: Permaculture principles can also be applied in semi-arid areas.\nStatement 4 – Correct: Mulching is an important permaculture practice for moisture conservation and soil protection.","revisionPoints":"Permaculture → sustainable, ecosystem-oriented agricultural design.\nEmphasis on diversity, soil health and resource conservation.\nMulching → conserves soil moisture and reduces evaporation.\nMonoculture → cultivation of a single crop over a large area.\nSustainable agriculture aims to balance productivity + ecological sustainability."},
{"no":52,"text":"With reference to 'palm oil', consider the following statements:\nThe palm oil tree is native to Southeast Asia.\nPalm oil is a raw material for industries producing lipstick and perfumes.\nPalm oil can be used to produce biodiesel.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Palm Oil","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Incorrect: The oil palm is originally from West Africa, although Southeast Asia is now a major production region.\nStatement 2 – Correct: Palm oil and its derivatives are used in cosmetics, including products such as lipstick and perfumes.\nStatement 3 – Correct: Palm oil can be processed into biodiesel.","revisionPoints":"Oil palm → origin associated with West/Central Africa.\nSoutheast Asia → major global oil-palm cultivation region.\nUses → food, cosmetics, soaps and industrial products.\nPalm oil can serve as a biofuel feedstock."},
{"no":53,"text":"With reference to the Indus river system, among Chenab, Jhelum, Ravi and Sutlej, which river joins the Indus directly?","A":"Chenab","B":"Jhelum","C":"Ravi","D":"Sutlej","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Indus River System","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"The Sutlej ultimately joins the Indus directly.\nThe Chenab, Jhelum and Ravi first join the combined river system before the flow reaches the Indus.","revisionPoints":"Indus system → Indus + major tributaries.\nMajor eastern tributaries → Sutlej, Beas, Ravi.\nWestern tributaries → Jhelum, Chenab.\nSutlej joins the Chenab, forming part of the combined flow that later enters the Indus.\nRiver-confluence questions require careful attention to the word \"directly.\""},
{"no":54,"text":"Didwana, Kuchaman, Sargol and Khatu are the names of:","A":"Glaciers","B":"Mangrove areas","C":"Ramsar sites","D":"Saline lakes","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Saline Lakes","Rajasthan","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Didwana, Kuchaman, Sargol and Khatu are saline lakes/depressions of Rajasthan.\nThey occur in the arid/semi-arid landscape and are associated with saline deposits.","revisionPoints":"Didwana and Kuchaman → Rajasthan.\nRajasthan has numerous salt lakes and saline depressions.\nSambhar → India's largest inland salt lake.\nArid climate + high evaporation → promotes salinity."},
{"no":55,"text":"Consider the following rivers:\nBrahmani\nNagavali\nSubarnarekha\nVamsadhara\nWhich of the above rise from the Eastern Ghats?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 and 4","C":"3 and 4","D":"1 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Eastern Ghats","Rivers of India","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Nagavali rises in the Eastern Ghats.\nVamsadhara also originates in the Eastern Ghats.\nBrahmani and Subarnarekha have different source regions.","revisionPoints":"Eastern Ghats → discontinuous mountain system along eastern India.\nNagavali → flows through Odisha and Andhra Pradesh.\nVamsadhara → important east-flowing river of Odisha–Andhra region.\nMany Eastern Ghats rivers ultimately drain into the Bay of Bengal."},
{"no":56,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nThe Global Ocean Commission grants licences for seabed exploration and mining in international waters.\nIndia has received licences for seabed mineral exploration in international waters.\nRare earth minerals are present on the seafloor in international waters.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"International Environmental Bodies","tags":["Seabed Mining","International Seabed Authority","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Incorrect: The Global Ocean Commission does not grant such licences.\nStatement 2 – Correct: India has received exploration rights/contracts for seabed minerals in international waters under the relevant international framework.\nStatement 3 – Correct: Seafloor deposits contain various minerals, including rare-earth-related resources.","revisionPoints":"International seabed beyond national jurisdiction → governed through international arrangements.\nInternational Seabed Authority (ISA) plays a key role in regulating mineral-related activities in the international seabed area.\nIndia has exploration interests in polymetallic nodules and other seabed resources.\nSeabed resources may contain strategic minerals."},
{"no":57,"text":"Among the following, which one is the least water-efficient crop?","A":"Sugarcane","B":"Sunflower","C":"Pearl millet","D":"Red gram","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Water-efficient Crops","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Sugarcane is a water-intensive crop.\nIt requires substantial water during its long growing period.\nPearl millet and red gram are comparatively more drought-tolerant.","revisionPoints":"Sugarcane → high water requirement.\nPearl millet → relatively drought-resistant.\nRed gram/pigeon pea → relatively suitable for dryland conditions.\nWater-efficient agriculture is important for water-stressed regions."},
{"no":58,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nIn the tropical zone, the western sections of the oceans are warmer than the eastern sections owing to the influence of trade winds.\nIn the temperate zone, westerlies make the eastern sections of oceans warmer than the western sections.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Ocean Currents","Trade Winds","Westerlies","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Correct: Trade winds push warm surface waters westward in tropical oceans, creating warmer western ocean regions.\nStatement 2 – Correct: Westerlies drive ocean circulation differently in temperate latitudes, contributing to warmer eastern ocean sectors relative to western sectors in the stated context.","revisionPoints":"Trade winds → generally east-to-west in the tropics.\nWesterlies → generally west-to-east in middle latitudes.\nOcean currents redistribute heat globally.\nWestern boundary currents such as the Gulf Stream/Kuroshio are generally warm and strong.\nOcean temperature patterns influence climate of adjacent land areas."},
{"no":59,"text":"In the context of India's preparation for Climate-Smart Agriculture, consider the following statements:\nThe 'Climate-Smart Village' approach in India is part of a project led by CCAFS.\nCCAFS is carried out under CGIAR headquartered in France.\nICRISAT is one of the CGIAR research centres.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Climate-Smart Agriculture","CGIAR","ICRISAT","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Correct: Climate-Smart Village approaches have been associated with CCAFS initiatives.\nStatement 2 – Correct: CCAFS operated under the CGIAR research framework.\nStatement 3 – Correct: ICRISAT is part of the CGIAR system.","revisionPoints":"CCAFS → Climate Change, Agriculture and Food Security.\nClimate-smart agriculture focuses on:\nproductivity,\nadaptation,\nmitigation.\nCGIAR → international agricultural research network.\nICRISAT → research focused especially on semi-arid tropics."},
{"no":60,"text":"\"Leaf litter decomposes faster than in any other biome...\" This description best fits:","A":"Coniferous forest","B":"Dry deciduous forest","C":"Mangrove forest","D":"Tropical rainforest","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Tropical Rainforest","Biomes","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Tropical rainforests have high temperature and abundant moisture throughout much of the year.\nThese conditions support intense microbial activity and rapid decomposition.\nNutrients are quickly released and rapidly taken up by vegetation.","revisionPoints":"Tropical rainforest → hot + wet conditions.\nHigh temperature + moisture → rapid decomposition.\nNutrient cycling is very rapid.\nDespite dense vegetation, tropical rainforest soils can be relatively nutrient-poor because nutrients are rapidly recycled and much is stored in biomass.\nConiferous forests generally have slower decomposition because of cooler conditions and resistant litter.\nUPSC 2021 – Questions 61–80"},
{"no":61,"text":"The vegetation of savannah is kept in check mainly by:\nBurrowing animals and termites\nFire\nGrazing herbivores\nSeasonal rainfall\nSoil properties","A":"1 and 2","B":"4 and 5","C":"2, 3 and 4","D":"1, 3 and 5","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Savannah Vegetation","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Fire periodically destroys excess grass and woody vegetation.\nGrazing herbivores control grass biomass.\nSeasonal rainfall limits vegetation growth and creates the characteristic grassland–woodland balance.\nTogether, these factors help maintain the savannah ecosystem.","revisionPoints":"Savannah → tropical/subtropical grassland with scattered trees.\nMajor controlling factors → fire, grazing and seasonal rainfall.\nWet and dry seasons are strongly pronounced.\nExcessive disturbance can alter the grassland–woodland balance."},
{"no":62,"text":"With reference to the water on the Earth, consider the following statements:\nWater in rivers and lakes is more than groundwater.\nWater in polar ice caps and glaciers is more than groundwater.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Hydrosphere","Water Distribution","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Incorrect: Groundwater contains substantially more freshwater than rivers and lakes.\nStatement 2 – Correct: Water stored in glaciers and ice caps is greater than groundwater.","revisionPoints":"Most of Earth's water → oceans.\nMost freshwater → stored in ice caps and glaciers.\nGroundwater is the next major freshwater reservoir.\nRivers and lakes contain only a very small fraction of Earth's total water."},
{"no":63,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nMoringa is a leguminous evergreen tree.\nTamarind tree is endemic to South Asia.\nIn India, most tamarind is collected as minor forest produce.\nIndia exports tamarind and seeds of moringa.\nSeeds of moringa and tamarind can be used in production of biofuels.","A":"1, 2, 4 and 5","B":"3, 4 and 5","C":"1, 3 and 4","D":"1, 2, 3 and 5","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Moringa","Tamarind","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Incorrect: Moringa belongs to the family Moringaceae, not the legume family.\nStatement 2 – Incorrect: Tamarind is not endemic exclusively to South Asia.\nStatement 3 – Correct: Tamarind is collected as a minor forest produce in India.\nStatement 4 – Correct: India exports tamarind and moringa seeds.\nStatement 5 – Correct: Oils from seeds can be considered for biofuel applications.","revisionPoints":"Moringa → commonly known as drumstick tree.\nTamarind → important food and agroforestry resource.\nMinor forest produce → non-timber forest products collected by forest-dependent communities.\nPlant seeds containing oils can serve as biofuel feedstocks."},
{"no":64,"text":"The black cotton soil of India has been formed due to weathering of:","A":"Brown forest soil","B":"Fissure volcanic rock","C":"Granite and schist","D":"Shale and limestone","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Black Cotton Soil","Deccan Trap","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Black cotton soil, or regur soil, is mainly associated with the weathering of basaltic lava.\nBasalt was extensively produced by ancient volcanic activity in the Deccan region.","revisionPoints":"Black soil → Deccan Trap region.\nParent material → basaltic/fissure volcanic rocks.\nRich in clay, especially montmorillonite.\nHigh moisture-retention capacity.\nParticularly suitable for cotton.\nDevelops deep cracks during dry periods."},
{"no":65,"text":"With reference to Recombinant Vector Vaccines, consider the following statements:\nGenetic engineering is applied in developing these vaccines.\nBacteria and viruses are used as vectors.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biotechnology","tags":["Recombinant Vector Vaccines","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Recombinant vector vaccines use genetic engineering to introduce selected genetic material into a vector.\nThe vector can be a virus or bacterium.\nThe vector delivers the genetic information that stimulates an immune response.","revisionPoints":"Recombinant vector vaccine → uses a carrier/vector organism.\nGenetic engineering → key technology.\nVector → carries genetic material encoding an antigen.\nImmune system responds to the antigen produced in the host."},
{"no":66,"text":"In the context of hereditary diseases:\nMitochondrial replacement therapy can prevent transmission of mitochondrial diseases.\nA child inherits mitochondrial diseases entirely from the mother.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Mitochondrial Replacement Therapy","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Correct: Mitochondrial replacement techniques can prevent transmission of certain mitochondrial DNA disorders.\nStatement 2 – Correct in the context of mitochondrial inheritance: Mitochondrial DNA is generally inherited from the mother.\nMitochondrial replacement involves replacing affected maternal mitochondria with healthy donor mitochondria.","revisionPoints":"Mitochondria contain their own DNA.\nMitochondrial DNA is generally maternally inherited.\nMitochondrial disorders can affect energy production.\nMitochondrial replacement therapy is sometimes referred to as the “three-parent baby” technique."},
{"no":67,"text":"Bollgard I and Bollgard II technologies are related to:","A":"Clonal propagation of crop plants","B":"Developing genetically modified crop plants","C":"Production of plant growth substances","D":"Production of biofertilizers","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biotechnology","tags":["Bollgard","GM Crops","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Bollgard technologies are varieties of genetically modified cotton.\nThey contain Bt genes that provide protection against certain insect pests.\nBollgard II contains additional Bt-related traits compared with the earlier technology.","revisionPoints":"Bollgard → Bt cotton technology.\nBt gene originally comes from Bacillus thuringiensis.\nBt cotton → insect-resistant GM crop.\nMain target → certain bollworm pests.\nGM crops involve introduction/modification of specific genetic traits."},
{"no":68,"text":"In a pressure cooker, the cooking temperature mainly depends upon:\nArea of the hole in the lid\nTemperature of the flame\nWeight of the lid","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Pressure Cooker","Physics","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"The pressure inside a pressure cooker depends on the pressure-control mechanism.\nThe area of the vent and the weight of the pressure regulator determine the pressure at which steam escapes.\nHigher pressure raises the boiling point of water and therefore increases the cooking temperature.\nThe flame mainly affects how quickly the cooker reaches the required pressure, rather than determining the regulated pressure.","revisionPoints":"Normal boiling point of water → 100°C at 1 atmosphere.\nPressure ↑ → boiling point ↑.\nPressure cooker → cooks food faster by increasing pressure and temperature.\nPressure regulator/vent controls excess pressure."},
{"no":69,"text":"Which of the following can be cultured in artificial/synthetic medium?\nBacteria\nFungi\nVirus","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Artificial Culture Medium","Virus","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Bacteria can generally be grown on artificial culture media.\nFungi can also be cultured on suitable artificial media.\nViruses require living host cells for replication and therefore cannot generally be grown on ordinary cell-free artificial media.","revisionPoints":"Bacteria → can grow on suitable culture media.\nFungi → can grow on artificial media.\nViruses → obligate intracellular parasites.\nVirus replication requires living host cells."},
{"no":70,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nAdenoviruses have single-stranded DNA genomes whereas retroviruses have double-stranded DNA genomes.\nCommon cold is sometimes caused by an adenovirus whereas AIDS is caused by a retrovirus.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Adenovirus","Retrovirus","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Incorrect: Adenoviruses contain double-stranded DNA, while retroviruses have single-stranded RNA genomes.\nStatement 2 – Correct: Adenoviruses can cause respiratory infections including some cases of common cold-like illness, while HIV, the cause of AIDS, is a retrovirus.","revisionPoints":"Adenovirus → double-stranded DNA virus.\nRetrovirus → single-stranded RNA virus.\nHIV → retrovirus.\nHIV uses reverse transcriptase to convert RNA information into DNA.\nAIDS → acquired immunodeficiency syndrome."},
{"no":71,"text":"Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid because:","A":"it is dipolar in nature","B":"it is a good conductor of heat","C":"it has high value of specific heat","D":"it is an oxide of hydrogen","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Water Chemistry","Solvent","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Water is a polar/dipolar molecule.\nIts partial positive and negative charges allow it to interact with and dissolve many ionic and polar substances.\nThis is why water is commonly called the “universal solvent.”","revisionPoints":"Water molecule → polar.\nPolarity results from unequal sharing of electrons and its molecular geometry.\nWater dissolves many ionic and polar compounds.\nNon-polar substances generally have low solubility in water."},
{"no":72,"text":"With reference to street-lighting, how do sodium lamps differ from LED lamps?\nSodium lamps produce light in 360° but LEDs do not.\nSodium lamps have longer lifespan than LEDs.\nSodium lamps produce almost monochromatic light while LEDs provide better colour rendering.","A":"3 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["Sodium Lamps","LED Lighting","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Correct: Conventional sodium lamps emit light broadly around the lamp, whereas LEDs are highly directional and can be designed to focus light.\nStatement 2 – Incorrect: LEDs generally have a longer operational lifespan than conventional sodium lamps.\nStatement 3 – Correct: Low-pressure sodium lamps produce nearly monochromatic yellow light, while LEDs generally provide better colour rendering.","revisionPoints":"Sodium lamps → characteristic yellow/orange light.\nLow-pressure sodium → nearly monochromatic.\nLEDs → semiconductor-based light sources.\nLEDs generally offer:\nlonger life,\nlower energy consumption,\ndirectional lighting,\nbetter colour options."},
{"no":73,"text":"The term 'ACE2' is talked about in the context of:","A":"genes introduced in GM plants","B":"India's satellite navigation system","C":"radio collars for wildlife tracking","D":"spread of viral diseases","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["ACE2","Viral Diseases","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"ACE2 stands for Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme 2.\nIt acts as a cellular receptor involved in the entry of SARS-CoV-2 into human cells.\nTherefore, ACE2 became important in discussions about COVID-19 and viral disease transmission.","revisionPoints":"ACE2 → cellular receptor/protein.\nSARS-CoV-2 uses ACE2 for cell entry.\nACE2 is part of the renin–angiotensin system.\nCOVID-19 → caused by SARS-CoV-2."},
{"no":74,"text":"Bisphenol-A (BPA) is a structural/key component in the manufacture of:","A":"Low-density polyethylene","B":"Polycarbonate","C":"Polyethylene terephthalate","D":"Polyvinyl chloride","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Materials Science","tags":["Bisphenol-A","Polycarbonate","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"BPA is an important raw material in the manufacture of polycarbonate plastics.\nIt is also used in some epoxy resins.","revisionPoints":"BPA → Bisphenol-A.\nImportant in production of polycarbonate.\nPolycarbonate → strong, lightweight and transparent plastic.\nBPA exposure has been studied because of its potential endocrine-disrupting effects."},
{"no":75,"text":"Triclosan is most likely present in:","A":"Food preservatives","B":"Fruit-ripening substances","C":"Reused plastic containers","D":"Toiletries","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Materials Science","tags":["Triclosan","Toiletries","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Triclosan is an antimicrobial chemical that has historically been used in certain personal-care and toiletry products.\nIt has been incorporated into products intended to reduce microbial growth.","revisionPoints":"Triclosan → antimicrobial/antibacterial compound.\nHistorically used in some soaps, toothpaste and personal-care products.\nIts environmental persistence and potential health effects have led to regulatory restrictions in various applications.\nDistinguish antimicrobial chemicals from ordinary preservatives."},
{"no":76,"text":"Why are astronomical distances measured in light-years?","A":"Distances among stellar bodies do not change.","B":"Gravity of stellar bodies does not change.","C":"Light always travels in a straight line.","D":"Speed of light is always the same.","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["Light-years","Astronomy","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"A light-year is a unit of distance, not time.\nIt is the distance travelled by light in one year.\nSince the speed of light in vacuum is constant, it provides a convenient standard for expressing enormous astronomical distances.","revisionPoints":"1 light-year ≈ 9.46 trillion km.\nLight-year → unit of distance.\n1 parsec ≈ 3.26 light-years.\nAstronomical distances are enormous, making light-years and parsecs convenient units."},
{"no":77,"text":"Parliamentary democracy based on the British model differs because:\nBritish Parliament is sovereign; in India Parliament's powers are limited.\nConstitutionality of Constitutional Amendments can be examined by the Supreme Court.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Parliamentary Sovereignty","Judicial Review","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Correct: Parliamentary sovereignty is a major principle of the British constitutional system, whereas India's Parliament is subject to the Constitution.\nStatement 2 – Correct: The Supreme Court can examine constitutional amendments under the basic structure doctrine.","revisionPoints":"UK → traditionally associated with parliamentary sovereignty.\nIndia → constitutional supremacy.\nParliament in India cannot destroy the Constitution's basic structure.\nBasic Structure Doctrine → Kesavananda Bharati case, 1973.\nJudicial review is an important feature of Indian constitutionalism."},
{"no":78,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nN. Gopalaswami Ayyangar Committee suggested a Minister and Secretary for Administrative Reforms.\nDepartment of Personnel was created in 1970 under the Prime Minister.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Public Administration","tags":["Gopalaswami Ayyangar Committee","Department of Personnel","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Incorrect: The statement about the Ayyangar Committee is not correct in the form given.\nStatement 2 – Correct: The Department of Personnel was created in 1970 under the Prime Minister.","revisionPoints":"Department of Personnel → important institution in personnel administration.\nIt deals with matters concerning the civil services.\nAdministrative reforms are connected with improving efficiency and accountability of government.\nThe Prime Minister's role has historically been important in central personnel administration."},
{"no":79,"text":"Right to Privacy is protected under:","A":"Article 15","B":"Article 19","C":"Article 21","D":"Article 29","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Fundamental Rights","tags":["Right to Privacy","Article 21","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"The Supreme Court recognized the Right to Privacy as a fundamental right under the Constitution.\nIn Justice K.S. Puttaswamy v. Union of India (2017), a nine-judge Bench affirmed privacy as an intrinsic part of life and personal liberty under Article 21.","revisionPoints":"Article 21 → Right to life and personal liberty.\nRight to Privacy → fundamental right.\nPuttaswamy judgment → 2017.\nPrivacy is connected with dignity, autonomy and personal liberty.\nFundamental rights can be subject to constitutionally valid restrictions."},
{"no":80,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nThere is no law restricting contesting from three Lok Sabha constituencies.\nIn 1991 Shri Devi Lal contested from three constituencies.\nUnder present rules, if a candidate contests from multiple constituencies, the political party bears the by-election cost.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3","D":"2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Elections","tags":["Multiple Constituencies","By-election","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Incorrect: Election law restricts a candidate from contesting from more than two constituencies in a general election.\nStatement 2 – Correct: Devi Lal contested from three constituencies in 1991, before the legal restriction was introduced.\nStatement 3 – Incorrect: The law does not simply place the by-election cost on the political party in the manner stated.","revisionPoints":"Representation of the People Act, 1951 → important election law.\nA candidate is presently restricted to contesting from two constituencies.\nMultiple-seat contests can create the need for a by-election when a candidate wins more than one seat.\nElection law has been amended over time to address multiple constituency contests.\nUPSC 2021 – Questions 81–100"},
{"no":81,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n“Right to the City” is an agreed human right monitored by UN-Habitat.\nIt gives every city occupant the right to reclaim public spaces and public participation.\nIt means State cannot deny public services to unauthorized colonies.","A":"1 only","B":"3 only","C":"1 and 2","D":"2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Local Self-Government","tags":["Right to the City","UN-Habitat","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Incorrect: “Right to the City” is not an internationally agreed human right formally monitored by UN-Habitat.\nStatement 2 – Correct: The concept emphasizes people's participation in urban life and access to public spaces.\nStatement 3 – Correct: It supports inclusive access to urban services, including for marginalized urban communities.","revisionPoints":"Right to the City → concept of inclusive and equitable urban development.\nFocus → participation, public spaces and access to urban opportunities.\nClosely related to inclusive urbanization.\nUN-Habitat works extensively on sustainable and inclusive cities."},
{"no":82,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nJudicial custody means the accused is in the custody of the Magistrate.\nDuring judicial custody, police cannot interrogate without court permission.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["Judicial Custody","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Incorrect: Judicial custody means the accused is placed in the custody of the judicial authority, generally in a jail, rather than being physically held by the Magistrate.\nStatement 2 – Correct: Police cannot ordinarily interrogate a person in judicial custody without obtaining the necessary permission of the court.","revisionPoints":"Police custody → accused remains under police control.\nJudicial custody → accused is under judicial authority and normally lodged in jail.\nPolice interrogation during judicial custody requires appropriate court permission.\nCustody and arrest are related but distinct legal concepts."},
{"no":83,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nIf a prisoner becomes eligible for parole, it cannot be denied.\nStates have their own Prisoners Release on Parole Rules.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["Parole","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Incorrect: Eligibility for parole does not create an absolute right to parole; it can be denied according to applicable rules and circumstances.\nStatement 2 – Correct: Prison administration is largely a State subject, and States have their own parole rules.","revisionPoints":"Prisons → State List under the Seventh Schedule.\nParole → temporary conditional release of a prisoner.\nParole rules can differ between States.\nParole ≠ acquittal or termination of sentence."},
{"no":84,"text":"The nodal ministry for implementation of the Forest Rights Act, 2006 is:","A":"Ministry of Environment, Forest & Climate Change","B":"Ministry of Panchayati Raj","C":"Ministry of Rural Development","D":"Ministry of Tribal Affairs","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Government Bodies","tags":["Forest Rights Act 2006","Ministry of Tribal Affairs","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"The Ministry of Tribal Affairs (MoTA) is the nodal ministry for implementation of the Forest Rights Act, 2006.\nThe Act recognizes the forest rights of forest-dwelling Scheduled Tribes and other traditional forest dwellers.","revisionPoints":"Forest Rights Act → 2006.\nFull name → Scheduled Tribes and Other Traditional Forest Dwellers (Recognition of Forest Rights) Act.\nNodal Ministry → Ministry of Tribal Affairs.\nGram Sabha has an important role in determining and recognizing forest rights."},
{"no":85,"text":"A law conferring unguided and uncontrolled discretionary power violates:","A":"Article 14","B":"Article 28","C":"Article 32","D":"Article 44","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Fundamental Rights","tags":["Article 14","Arbitrary Discretion","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Article 14 guarantees equality before law and equal protection of laws.\nArbitrary and unguided discretionary power can result in unequal and arbitrary treatment.\nTherefore, excessive or uncontrolled discretion can violate the principle of equality.","revisionPoints":"Article 14 → Equality before law + equal protection of laws.\nArbitrary State action can be challenged under Article 14.\nRule of law requires government power to operate within legal and constitutional limits.\nArticle 32 → constitutional remedies, not equality."},
{"no":86,"text":"Which feature indicates that Indian polity is federal?","A":"Independence of judiciary","B":"Union Legislature elected from constituent units","C":"Union Cabinet with regional representatives","D":"Fundamental Rights enforceable by courts","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Federalism","Indian Polity","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"An independent judiciary is an important feature of a federal system.\nIn a federation, disputes between different levels of government require an impartial constitutional authority.\nIndia's judiciary can adjudicate disputes involving the Union and States.","revisionPoints":"Federal features of India include:\nconstitutional division of powers,\nwritten Constitution,\nsupremacy of Constitution,\nindependent judiciary.\nIndia also has strong unitary features.\nUnion and State powers are distributed through the Seventh Schedule.\nSupreme Court acts as an important constitutional umpire."},
{"no":87,"text":"Which one best defines the term State?","A":"Community occupying a territory with organized government","B":"Politically organized people of a definite territory possessing authority to govern","C":"Persons living in a territory sharing culture","D":"Society with central authority and judiciary","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Political Theory","tags":["Definition of State","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"A State is generally understood through essential elements such as:\npopulation,\nterritory,\ngovernment,\nsovereignty.\nAmong the given options, option (a) best corresponds to the concept used in the source.","revisionPoints":"Essential elements of a State → population, territory, government and sovereignty.\nGovernment is the machinery through which State authority is exercised.\nState ≠ government.\nNation generally refers to a community with shared identity, while State is a political-legal entity."},
{"no":88,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nA retired Supreme Court judge can be requested to sit in the Supreme Court with approval of the President.\nHigh Courts have review powers similar to the Supreme Court.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["Supreme Court","High Courts","Review Powers","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Correct: Under Article 128, a retired Supreme Court judge may be requested to sit and act as a judge of the Supreme Court with the required consent/approval.\nStatement 2 – Incorrect: The constitutional position and scope of review powers of High Courts are not identical to those of the Supreme Court.","revisionPoints":"Article 128 → attendance of retired Supreme Court judges.\nSupreme Court → court of record under Article 129.\nHigh Courts → courts of record under Article 215.\nJudicial review allows courts to examine the constitutional validity of laws and executive actions."},
{"no":89,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nIndia has only one citizenship and one domicile.\nOnly a citizen by birth can become the Head of State.\nCitizenship granted to a foreigner cannot be taken away.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3","D":"2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Citizenship","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Correct: India follows single citizenship. The source treats India as having one domicile in the constitutional sense.\nStatement 2 – Incorrect: A person who is not a citizen by birth can also become President if constitutionally eligible.\nStatement 3 – Incorrect: Citizenship acquired through registration or naturalisation can be terminated under the Citizenship Act.","revisionPoints":"India → single citizenship.\nPresident → must be an Indian citizen, but citizenship need not be acquired by birth.\nCitizenship can be lost through:\nrenunciation,\ntermination,\ndeprivation.\nCitizenship is primarily governed by Articles 5–11 and the Citizenship Act, 1955."},
{"no":90,"text":"The best safeguard of liberty in a liberal democracy is:","A":"Committed judiciary","B":"Centralization of powers","C":"Elected government","D":"Separation of powers","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Political Theory","tags":["Separation of Powers","Liberal Democracy","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Separation of powers prevents excessive concentration of authority.\nIt creates institutional checks among the legislature, executive and judiciary.\nSuch checks help protect individual liberty.","revisionPoints":"Separation of powers → distribution of governmental functions.\nChecks and balances → prevent concentration of power.\nJudicial independence → important for protecting constitutional rights.\nLiberal democracy emphasizes individual liberty and limited government."},
{"no":91,"text":"Under the Indian Constitution, concentration of wealth violates:","A":"Right to Equality","B":"Directive Principles of State Policy","C":"Right to Freedom","D":"Concept of Welfare","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Directive Principles of State Policy","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"The Constitution directs the State to minimize inequalities in income, status, facilities and opportunities.\nArticle 39(b) and 39(c) specifically deal with distribution of material resources and prevention of concentration of wealth.","revisionPoints":"Article 38 → promote welfare and reduce inequalities.\nArticle 39(b) → distribution of material resources for the common good.\nArticle 39(c) → prevent concentration of wealth and means of production.\nDPSPs are not generally enforceable by courts, but are fundamental in governance."},
{"no":92,"text":"What is the position of the Right to Property in India?","A":"Legal right available to citizens only","B":"Directive Principles of State Policy","C":"Fundamental Right available to citizens only","D":"Neither Fundamental Right nor legal right","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Fundamental Rights","tags":["Right to Property","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Source answer: (b).\nHowever, the key constitutional point to remember is that the Right to Property is not a Fundamental Right today.\nArticle 300A provides that no person shall be deprived of property except by authority of law.\nTherefore, it is a constitutional/legal right, rather than a DPSP.","revisionPoints":"44th Constitutional Amendment, 1978 → removed Right to Property from Fundamental Rights.\nArticle 300A → constitutional protection of property.\nRight to Property today → constitutional/legal right.\nIt is not a Fundamental Right.\nImportant: the source's marked option should be treated as its supplied key; the constitutional position is distinct."},
{"no":93,"text":"What was the exact constitutional status of India on 26th January, 1950?","A":"A Democratic Republic","B":"A Sovereign Democratic Republic","C":"A Sovereign Secular Democratic Republic","D":"A Sovereign Socialist Secular Democratic Republic","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Preamble","Sovereign Democratic Republic","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"On 26 January 1950, the Constitution came into force.\nThe original Preamble described India as a Sovereign Democratic Republic.\nThe words “Socialist” and “Secular” were added later by the 42nd Constitutional Amendment, 1976.","revisionPoints":"Constitution adopted → 26 November 1949.\nConstitution came into force → 26 January 1950.\nOriginal Preamble → Sovereign Democratic Republic.\n42nd Amendment, 1976 → added Socialist, Secular and changed some wording.\n“Integrity” was also added to the Preamble by the 42nd Amendment."},
{"no":94,"text":"Constitutional Government means:","A":"A representative government of a nation with federal structure","B":"A government whose Head enjoys nominal powers","C":"A government whose Head enjoys real powers","D":"A government limited by the terms of the Constitution","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Political Theory","tags":["Constitutional Government","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Constitutional government means governmental power is limited and regulated by the Constitution.\nGovernment authorities cannot exercise unlimited or arbitrary power.\nConstitutionalism emphasizes limited government, rule of law and protection of rights.","revisionPoints":"Constitutional government → government limited by constitutional rules.\nRule of law → government itself is subject to law.\nConstitutionalism → limits arbitrary exercise of State power.\nWritten Constitution provides a framework for governmental authority."},
{"no":95,"text":"With reference to India, the terms 'Halbi, Ho and Kui' pertain to:","A":"Dance forms of North-West India","B":"Musical instruments","C":"Pre-historic cave paintings","D":"Tribal languages","ans":"D","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Festivals & Traditions","tags":["Tribal Languages","Halbi","Ho","Kui","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Halbi, Ho and Kui are languages associated with tribal communities of India.\nThey belong to different linguistic groups and are spoken mainly in parts of central and eastern India.","revisionPoints":"Halbi → spoken in parts of Chhattisgarh, Odisha and Maharashtra.\nHo → associated particularly with the Ho tribal community.\nKui → associated with the Kandha/Kondh community, especially Odisha.\nTribal languages reflect India's linguistic diversity."},
{"no":96,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding Bharat Ratna and Padma Awards:\nBharat Ratna and Padma Awards are titles under Article 18(1).\nPadma Awards, instituted in 1954, were suspended only once.\nBharat Ratna Awards are restricted to a maximum of five in a year.\nWhich statements are not correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["Bharat Ratna","Padma Awards","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Not correct: National awards such as Bharat Ratna and Padma Awards are not treated as titles prohibited by Article 18.\nStatement 2 – Not correct: Padma Awards were not simply suspended only once; they were discontinued for a period and later revived.\nStatement 3 – Not correct: There is no constitutional restriction limiting Bharat Ratna to exactly five awards per year.","revisionPoints":"Bharat Ratna → India's highest civilian award.\nPadma awards:\nPadma Vibhushan\nPadma Bhushan\nPadma Shri\nArticle 18 → abolition of titles.\nNational awards are distinguished from hereditary or honorific titles.\nBharat Ratna can be awarded to a maximum of three persons in a particular year under the relevant rules, rather than five."},
{"no":97,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement 1: UNCDF and Arbor Day Foundation recognized Hyderabad as the 2020 Tree City of the World.Statement 2: Hyderabad was selected because of its commitment to grow and maintain urban forests.","A":"Both statements are correct and Statement 2 is the correct explanation.","B":"Both statements are correct but Statement 2 is not the correct explanation.","C":"Statement 1 is correct but Statement 2 is incorrect.","D":"Statement 1 is incorrect but Statement 2 is correct.","ans":"D","category":"Current Affairs","subcategory":"Awards & Honours","tags":["Tree City of the World","Hyderabad","UNCDF","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Incorrect: Hyderabad was recognized by the Arbor Day Foundation and FAO, not UNCDF, in the cited context.\nStatement 2 – Correct: The recognition related to Hyderabad's efforts toward urban forestry and maintaining green spaces.","revisionPoints":"Tree Cities recognition → associated with the Arbor Day Foundation and FAO.\nUrban forestry → trees and green infrastructure within cities.\nBenefits include:\ntemperature regulation,\nair-quality improvement,\nbiodiversity,\ncarbon storage."},
{"no":98,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the Laureus World Sports Awards:\nTiger Woods was the first winner.\nFormula One drivers have received the award most often.\nRoger Federer has won the award the maximum number of times.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Current Affairs","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["Laureus World Sports Awards","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Correct: Tiger Woods was the inaugural winner of the Laureus World Sportsman of the Year Award.\nStatement 2 – Incorrect: Formula One drivers do not constitute the group that has received the award most often.\nStatement 3 – Correct: Roger Federer has won the award the maximum number of times.","revisionPoints":"Laureus World Sports Awards → major international sports awards.\nTiger Woods → inaugural Sportsman of the Year winner.\nRoger Federer → record number of Sportsman of the Year awards.\nAwards cover multiple sports and categories."},
{"no":99,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the 32nd Summer Olympics (Tokyo 2020):\nThe official motto was \"A New World.\"\nSport Climbing, Surfing, Skateboarding, Karate and Baseball were included.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Current Affairs","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["Tokyo Olympics 2020","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Incorrect: The official Olympic motto for Tokyo 2020 was “United by Emotion.”\nStatement 2 – Correct: Sport climbing, surfing, skateboarding and karate were introduced, while baseball/softball returned to the Olympic programme.","revisionPoints":"Tokyo Olympics → held in 2021, although officially Tokyo 2020.\nOfficial motto → United by Emotion.\nNew sports included:\nsport climbing,\nsurfing,\nskateboarding,\nkarate.\nBaseball and softball also returned to the programme."},
{"no":100,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the ICC World Test Championship:\nFinalists were decided by the number of matches won.\nNew Zealand ranked ahead of England because it won more matches.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Current Affairs","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["ICC World Test Championship","UPSC","2021"],"expl":"Statement 1 – Incorrect: The World Test Championship qualification was based on the percentage of points won (PCT) under the relevant system, not simply the number of matches won.\nStatement 2 – Incorrect: New Zealand's position was not determined simply by winning more matches than England.","revisionPoints":"ICC World Test Championship → championship competition for Test cricket.\nQualification uses points/PCT, rather than simply total wins.\nDifferent series can involve different numbers of matches.\nThe 2021 inaugural final was played between India and New Zealand.\nNew Zealand won the inaugural WTC final."},
];
